<MakerLayout title="How to Split People into Fair Random Teams — PARADOX Makers" description="A practical guide to random team assignment for classrooms, sports, workplaces, parties and events."><main class="maker-main"><article class="maker-why"><div class="maker-label">GUIDE · RANDOM TEAMS</div><h1>How to split a group into fair random teams</h1><p>Random grouping is useful when you want a quick decision without arguing over who should work with whom. The trick is to make the process transparent, repeatable and easy to check.</p><h2>Start with one name per line</h2><p>Clean input makes a clean result. Put every participant on their own line and remove duplicates before generating. For a classroom, use the roster exactly as you want it displayed.</p><h2>Pick the number of teams first</h2><p>Think about the task before the names. Three teams of five may be ideal for a fifteen-person activity; two teams of seven may work better for a debate. The generator then distributes the shuffled names across the chosen number of groups.</p><h2>Why randomness feels fair</h2><p>When no participant controls the assignment, everyone starts from the same rule. A random draw does not guarantee equal skill or equal experience, but it does remove the appearance that one person hand-picked the groups.</p><h2>Re-run when the activity changes</h2><p>Use a new assignment for a new round. This keeps teams from becoming permanent and can help students or coworkers collaborate with different people over time.</p><h2>Keep sensitive lists private</h2><p>A browser-side generator is useful when the list contains names you do not want uploaded to a randomization service. PARADOX Makers processes the core assignment locally in the browser.</p><p><a href="/random-team-generator/">Split a group now →</a></p></article></main></MakerLayout>
